From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 03:05:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: Dolby Vision tunnel: refuse its frames once
 a read back of its pipe was not as it needs

A read back of the pipe of a Dolby Vision tunnel that was not as the
tunnel needs (DCE 11.2 and 12, DCN 4.2 and 4.2B) left the video that was
playing in the tunnel: on DCN 4.2 only the next commit that turned the
tunnel on was refused, and DCE only wrote its read back to the kernel
log. That video went on through a pipe that was found not to be bit
exact, to its end.

Now the verdict of such a read back, or of one that could not be made
(amdgpu_dm_dvbridge_readback_verdict()), refuses every commit whose
active stream carries the tunnel, the frames of the video playing among
them (amdgpu_dm_dvbridge_readback_refuses(), first of the checks of
amdgpu_dm_dvbridge_check_scanout()): each frame commits the primary
plane, which brings its CRTC into the commit. User space that shows its
frames with strict commits stops showing them and turns the tunnel off
(Kodi: after 24 frames over two seconds that could not be shown); that
commit, whose stream no longer carries the tunnel, is not refused, so
the same video goes on in the ordinary output (HDR10). The tunnel stays
refused until the driver is loaded again, on DCE and on DCN 4.2 alike.
The kernel log says it with the read back:

  Dolby Vision tunnel, DCN 4.2: read back NOT as the tunnel needs (...);
  every commit of the tunnel is refused from now on, its frames among
  them, until the driver is loaded again: the video goes on in the
  ordinary output

A refused frame says why too. A commit of the frames has no connector in
its state, so amdgpu_dm_dvbridge_refused_scanout() takes the connectors
of the CRTC in (drm_atomic_add_affected_connectors(), with the
connection mutex under which DVBRIDGE_REFUSED is written and read) and
gives -EDEADLK back to the atomic check when it has to back off; that
holds for every refusal of the scanout of a frame, which said nothing
before. DVBRIDGE_REFUSED then says "scanout", and the kernel log says
that a read back of the pipe refused it.

Host test: the two functions, with amdgpu_dm_dvbridge_stream_active(),
cut out of the kernel file onto mock types (the Dolby VSIF, its HDMI
1.4b form, HDR over the tunnel, and streams that differ from those in
one byte); their callers checked as text, with each break of that
wiring caught.
